<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="avenanthramide biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The avenanthramides are a group of &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-cinnamoylanthranilic acids comprising |FRAME: ANTHRANILATE| and |FRAME: CPD-674| linked by an amide bond |CITS: [Collins88]|. The avenanthramides were first identified as phytoalexins in oat leaves infected by the pathogenic fungus |FRAME: TAX-27344| |CITS: [Tani81]|, but were later found at significant levels in oat grains |CITS: [Collins89][21598950]|. They were also detected in white cabbage butterfly (|FRAME: TAX-7116|) eggs |CITS: [7964796]| and in fungus-infected carnation (|FRAME: TAX-3570|) |CITS: [Chu13]|. More than 25 different types of avenanthramides have been detected in oat grains, differing by the various substituted groups on the two main components. The three most abundant ones comprise |FRAME: CPD-23407| conjugated with |FRAME: COUMARATE| (|FRAME: CPD-23404|), |FRAME: FERULIC-ACID| (|FRAME: CPD-23405|), or |FRAME: CPD-676| (|FRAME: CPD-23406|) |CITS: [Collins89]|. Avenanthramides were shown to have anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, anti-itch, anti-irritant, and antiatherogenic activities |CITS: [17373175][21417395]|. They are currently used commercially for skin, hair, baby, and sun care products. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The first key enzyme in the biosynthesis of the avenanthramides to be studied was |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.302| (HHT), an anthranilate acyl-CoA dependent acyltransferase that is homologous to |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.133| |CITS: [Ishihara97][14714871][16616872][26053460]|. The acyl donors used by HHT are diverged from the phenylpropanoid pathway and their biosynthesis is predicted to require |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.12| |CITS: [4364250][Hahlbrock89][12242399]|. A 2019 study has provided additional information about the pathway |CITS: [31394723]|. The authors cloned the genes encoding |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.12|, |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.302|, and |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.104|, and used the purified proteins to verify the predicted activities. According to the proposed pathway, |FRAME: COUMARATE| is initially derived from |FRAME: PHE| by the combined action of |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.24| (PAL) and |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.91| (C4&apos;H). It is activated into its CoA thioester by |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.12| (4CL), and the product is condensed by HHT with |FRAME: CPD-23407| to form |FRAME: CPD-23404|. In a parallel branch, |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA| can be converted to |FRAME: CPD-412| or |FRAME: 4-COUMAROYLQUINATE| by |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.133| (HCT), followed by hydroxylation by |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.96|, a cytochrome P450 enzyme (CYP98). A thioesterase reaction catalyzed by HCT can then release |FRAME: CAFFEOYL-COA|, which is condensed by HHT with |FRAME: CPD-23407| to form |FRAME: CPD-23406|. Finally, |FRAME: CPD-23406| can be methylated into |FRAME: CPD-23405| by |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.104| |CITS: [31394723]|.</Description>
